UNLINK(2)                      P32W Unix                       UNLINK(2)

NAME
       unlink - remove a directory entry

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int unlink(const char *path);

DESCRIPTION
       unlink removes the entry path from its directory and lowers
       the file's link count. When the count reaches zero and no
       process has the file open, the file's blocks and inode are
       freed; while it is still open it lives on, nameless, until
       the last close(2).

       The directory holding the entry must be writable. If the
       directory has the sticky bit (01000) set, only the file's
       owner or the super-user may remove it. Directories are
       removed with rmdir(2).

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENOENT  The file does not exist, or a directory in path
               does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path; or the directory is not writable; or it is
               sticky and the caller does not own the file.
       EISDIR  path is a directory.
       EINVAL  The last component of path is . or ..
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       rmdir(2), link(2), rename(2), close(2), rm(1)

BUGS
       Unlike V7, not even the super-user can unlink a directory.
